From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: TBI Lab runtime <runtime@tbi-lab.local>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 22:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] hw/gpio/esp32_gpio, hw/i2c/esp32_i2c, hw/misc/tbi_bridge,
 hw/xtensa: undefined-input reads, GPIO edge interrupts, I2C clock (Runtime
 v5)

TBI Lab (Sprint 25). Three bridge capabilities.

input-read: the host marks pads whose level it cannot define (Z <pad> <0|1>: nothing holds the
net, or its voltage is between V_IL and V_IH with no previous level). A guest read of GPIO_IN or
GPIO_IN1 while such a pad of that register is not driving is reported once, at the read itself, as
N <vns> <mask> (bit N = GPIO N); a pad is reported again only after the host defined it in between.
The value read is unchanged: the host ends the session instead of letting it be used.

gpio-irq: edge interrupts (ESP32 TRM v5.9, Register 6.30: GPIO_PINn_INT_TYPE 1 rising, 2 falling,
3 any edge; INT_ENA bit 0 APP CPU, bit 2 PRO CPU). A change of a pad's GPIO_IN bit that matches
its type sets its GPIO_STATUS/STATUS1 bit while the PRO or APP CPU interrupt is enabled;
GPIO_PCPU_INT/ACPU_INT(1) read the status bits enabled for that CPU, and each CPU's
GPIO_INTERRUPT line is high while one is set. The interrupt matrix gains per-CPU source inputs
(the GPIO signal differs per CPU on ETS_GPIO_INTR_SOURCE), whose level follows a later map change.
Level types (the TRM does not state how a cleared status behaves while the level persists), NMI
and SDIO enables and wake-up enable stay reported as unmodelled gpio-interrupt.

i2c-clock: each START frame carries the SCL period programmed then, in 80 MHz module clock cycles,
as TRM v5.9 Table 21.3-1 defines it from SCL_LOW_PERIOD, SCL_HIGH_PERIOD and I2C_SCL_FILTER_CFG
(now stored): B <vns> <bus> S <addr> <rw> <cycles>. The host checks it against the addressed
part's rated clock. Rise time is not included, so the frequency derived is an upper bound.
